cript date"}],["$","dd",null,{"children":["$","time",null,{"dateTime":"2026-09-23","children":"2026-09-23"}]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","dt",null,{"children":"Length"}],["$","dd",null,{"children":[36," pages"]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","dt",null,{"children":"Type"}],["$","dd",null,{"children":"Research paper"}]]}]]}],["$","a",null,{"className":"paper-read-primary","href":"/papers/AIRP-V3ZR-2MYB-U2ZZ/v3.1.0.pdf","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer","aria-label":"Read PDF in a new tab: Planar matching under monotone pairwise angles","children":["Read PDF ",["$","span",null,{"children":["v","3.1.0"," ↗"]}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"paper-preview-note","children":"Opens in a new tab."}],false]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"paper-reading-body","children":[["$","section",null,{"aria-labelledby":"abstract-title","children":[["$","h2",null,{"id":"abstract-title","children":"Overview"}],[["$","p","For periodic collision-free planar target motions with nonincreasing pairwise relative angles, the paper bounds the number of uniquely optimal squared-distance matchings by max(1, 2W), where W is total clockwise pair winding. The same bound holds for a coordinate-lexicographic canonical selection. Rigid rotation has the sharp count n(n − 1).",{"children":"For periodic collision-free planar target motions with nonincreasing pairwise relative angles, the paper bounds the number of uniquely optimal squared-distance matchings by max(1, 2W), where W is total clockwise pair winding. The same bound holds for a coordinate-lexicographic canonical selection. Rigid rotation has the sharp count n(n − 1)."}],["$","p","For densely encoded rational Fourier trajectories, an exact algorithm checks the geometric hypotheses and solves registration without a genericity assumption. Counterexamples separate angular monotonicity from winding alone and show a three-dimensional obstruction. Further results treat specified small mass perturbations and a sharp single-defect transport bound.",{"children":"For densely encoded rational Fourier trajectories, an exact algorithm checks the geometric hypotheses and solves registration without a genericity assumption. Counterexamples separate angular monotonicity from winding alone and show a three-dimensional obstruction. Further results treat specified small mass perturbations and a sharp single-defect transport bound."}]],"$Ld"]}],"$Le","$Lf","$undefined","$L10","$L11","$L12",false]}]]}]]}],"$L13"]
